var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"sas/#SAS-PROC-LOGISTIC","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"This page shows how a SAS PROC LOGISTIC analysis with a STRATA statement (the conditional logistic regression in, for example, SAS 9.3) maps onto ConditionalLogit.jl, and where the two can differ. ConditionalLogit.jl's defaults follow SAS, so this is the comparison that needs the fewest options. See Comparison with SAS, Stata, R and Python for the other programs.","title":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Which-model","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement maximises the exact conditional likelihood\n\nL_k(beta) = fracexpleft(sum_i in textevents_k x_ibetaright)sum_S subset textstratum  k S = m_k expleft(sum_j in S x_jbetaright)\n\nfor any number of events mk per stratum. It does not use the Breslow or Efron approximation. This is also what R survival::clogit(method = \"exact\") and Stata clogit compute. This package computes the same likelihood with no approximation. (R's clogit default is method = \"exact\", but coxph-based code that uses Breslow or Efron ties gives different numbers when mk > 1.)","title":"Which model"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Response-ordering:-check-the-signs","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"By default SAS models the probability of the lower ordered response value. For a 0/1 outcome that is Y = 0, unless the code says model y(event='1') = ... or model y(descending) = ....\n\nSAS code Julia\nmodel y(event='1') = x; or model y(descending) = x; clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id) (default)\nmodel y = x; with y coded 0/1 clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id, event = 0)\nmodel status(event='case') = x; clogit(@formula(status ~ x), df; strata = :id, event = \"case\")\n\nChanging the event level negates every coefficient (β → −β) and inverts every odds ratio. Standard errors, Wald χ², p-values, −2 Log L, AIC, SC and all three global tests are unchanged. If a paper's code shows no EVENT='1' or DESCENDING, negate the published estimates (or invert its odds ratios) before comparing, or fit with event = 0. With an offset, a flipped model is not a pure sign flip. For character responses SAS orders the levels alphabetically (formatted values).","title":"Response ordering: check the signs"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#CLASS-variables","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC defaults to PARAM=EFFECT (deviation coding) with the last level as reference. StatsModels defaults to dummy coding with the first level as base. To reproduce the SAS parameter table, pass contrasts = Dict(:g => EffectsCoding(base = \"<last level>\")). For PARAM=REF, pass DummyCoding(base = \"<last level>\"). You will need using StatsModels. oddsratios(m) reports level-versus-reference odds ratios computed from the contrast matrix, so they match SAS under either coding. Under effect coding, exp(β) is not an odds ratio.","title":"CLASS variables"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Convergence","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"As in SAS, the fit:\n\nstarts at β = 0;\nuses Newton–Raphson;\nstops when the relative gradient criterion g'I⁻¹g / (|ℓ| + 1e-6) < gconv = 1e-8;\nreports that iterate, without taking one more step.\n\nThe reported estimates can therefore differ from the exact optimum in about the 6th significant digit. SAS prints 4 decimals, so this does not show. For full accuracy, pass gconv = 1e-16. Non-convergence after maxiter = 25 iterations, and complete or quasi-complete separation, give a warning and converged(m) == false, as SAS gives a warning and still prints the tables. Separation is detected with direction certificates (events at least as high as non-events in every stratum along a direction). A stratum counts as a tie only within rounding error of its own values, and every certificate is confirmed by evaluating ℓ along it, so an outlying covariate value does not raise a false alarm.","title":"Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Step-control-differs-from-SAS","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"SAS labels the conditional fit \"Newton-Raphson ridge\" (NRRIDG), which handles a step that lowers ℓ by increasing a ridge on the Hessian. This package halves the step instead, and ridges only when the information matrix is not positive definite. When the full Newton step is accepted, which is the usual case from β = 0, the iterates are the same. On hard problems (near-separation, poor start values) the iterates, and hence the last digits of the reported GCONV iterate, can differ from SAS. The report therefore says \"Newton-Raphson (step-halving; ridge if not PD)\".","title":"Step control differs from SAS"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Fit-statistics","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"−2 Log L \"Without Covariates\" is −2ℓ(0). It equals 2Σk log C(nk, m_k) when there is no offset.\nAIC = −2 Log L + 2p.\nSC = −2 Log L + p·log(n). Here n is the number of observations (sum of FREQ), not the number of strata. The LOGIEX11 output confirms this: 83.654 + log(126) = 88.490.\n\nOne detail is unverified against SAS: whether SAS counts observations in uninformative strata (all events or no events) in this n. LOGIEX11 has no uninformative strata, so it cannot settle the question. The default sc_n = :informative excludes them, and sc_n = :all includes them. The two agree whenever every stratum is informative. When they differ, the report prints a note giving the n used in SC, because the \"Number of Observations Used\" lines include uninformative strata. If you have SAS access, one run of a model with an all-event stratum would settle this.","title":"Fit statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Strata","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"Strata with only events or only non-events are uninformative. They are reported in the Strata Summary and not used, as in SAS. Rows with a missing value in the response, a covariate, the strata, FREQ or the offset are dropped. A missing stratum id is kept as its own stratum only with strata_missing = true.","title":"Strata"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#FREQ-and-WEIGHT","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"FREQ: each row counts as freq identical observations. Non-integer values are truncated, and rows with freq < 1 are dropped, as in SAS.\nWEIGHT: SAS does not allow it with STRATA. The package therefore rejects pweights and aweights.","title":"FREQ and WEIGHT"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Collinearity","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"A covariate that is a linear combination of earlier covariates within strata is set to 0 with DF 0, with a warning. This includes a covariate that is constant within every stratum, which is collinear with the conditioned-out stratum intercepts. The later column is the one dropped, as in SAS and R. SAS's default CHECKDEPENDENCY=COVARIATES does not detect the stratum-constant case, so our behaviour corresponds to CHECKDEPENDENCY=ALL.\n\nThe default tolerance singular = 1e7·eps ≈ 2.2e-9 is the SAS SINGULAR= default. A column is dropped when 1 − R² on the earlier columns, computed from the within-stratum centred Gram matrix, is below it. SAS instead compares a sweep pivot of the Hessian with SINGULAR × a norm of the matrix. The null space is the same, but nearly collinear covariates close to the threshold can be decided differently.","title":"Collinearity"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Not-supported","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"The SAS EXACT statement (exact conditional inference).\nProfile-likelihood intervals (CLODDS=PL, CLPARM=PL).\nFirth's penalised likelihood.\npredict(m, newdata; type = :prob) for strata with more than one event (it gives one-event choice probabilities). residuals(m) is not defined.\nBreslow and Efron approximations.","title":"Not supported"},{"category":"section","location":"sas/#Cost-with-large-FREQ-counts","page":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC","text":"The exact likelihood costs O(nk · min(mk, nk − mk)) per stratum, where nk counts FREQ copies. Aggregated tables with very large cell counts therefore take time proportional to the counts, not to the number of rows. Memory stays O(rows · mk).","title":"Cost with large FREQ counts"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"ConditionalLogit.jl is checked against published output from SAS and Stata, against R survival::clogit(method = \"exact\"), and against independent internal oracles.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#SAS","page":"Validation","text":"Every number printed in the SAS/STAT documentation for the matched-pairs example LOGIEX11 is reproduced at SAS's printed precision with the default settings: Model Fit Statistics, the three global tests, the parameter estimates and the odds ratios (test test/test_sas.jl).","title":"SAS"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Stata","page":"Validation","text":"The three model fits in the Stata [R] clogit manual entry are reproduced to every digit Stata prints, using gconv = 1e-16:\n\nExample Data What is checked\n1 clogitid (369 obs, up to 2 positive outcomes per group) coefficients, std. errors, z, CIs, Number of obs, LR chi2, Log likelihood, Pseudo R2\n2 lowbirth2 (56 matched pairs, factor variable i.race) the same, for 7 parameters\n4 union (26,200 obs, fixed-effects panel; 2,744 groups dropped, black omitted) the same, plus Number of obs = 12,035 and the omitted variable\n\nThe weighted McNemar example (example 3, frequency weights on groups) is reproduced on the Stata page.\n\nThe script validation/stata_manual.R downloads the Stata Press data sets and validation/stata_manual.jl runs the comparison. It is not part of Pkg.test(), because it needs the network (commands below).\n\nWith the SAS default gconv = 1e-8, 2–4 coefficients per example differ from Stata in the last of the seven printed digits, because Stata takes one more Newton step (see Stata clogit).\n\nFull precision. validation/stata_manual.do reruns the three examples in Stata (checked with Stata/MP 15). It saves the results at full precision, with Stata's default convergence settings and with tight ones (nrtolerance(1e-12) tolerance(1e-14)). stata_manual.jl then compares them with ConditionalLogit.jl (gconv = 1e-16):\n\nExample Stata settings max |Δβ| max |Δ se| |Δℓ| |Δℓ(0)|\nclogitid default 7e-10 9e-12 0 1e-14\nlowbirth2 default 3e-11 8e-12 2e-14 7e-15\nunion default 9e-11 1e-12 0 6e-12\nclogitid tight 5e-12 2e-14 0 1e-14\nlowbirth2 tight 8e-15 3e-15 2e-14 7e-15\nunion tight 9e-11 1e-12 0 6e-12\n\nThe agreement in ℓ(0) confirms that Stata's e(ll_0) is the conditional log-likelihood at β = 0, nullloglikelihood(m).\n\nRscript validation/stata_manual.R                    # downloads the data (.dta and .csv)\n(cd validation && stata-mp -b do stata_manual.do)    # optional: Stata's full-precision results\njulia --project=docs validation/stata_manual.jl","title":"Stata"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#R","page":"Validation","text":"R survival 3.8-6, clogit(method = \"exact\"):\n\nFixtures (test/test_reference.jl, generated by test/reference/generate_reference.R): infert; LOGIEX11 with gall + hyper; synthetic data with m_k > 1, unequal stratum sizes, uninformative strata, string ids, a categorical covariate, an offset, missing values, event = 0, large strata (n = 200, m up to 170), and FREQ (checked against R on row-expanded data).\nWith gconv = 1e-16, coefficients agree to ≤ 2e-9 absolute (most ≤ 1e-11), and log-likelihoods to ≤ 5e-12.\nAt a fixed β, the log-likelihood, score statistic and covariance agree to ≤ 2e-14 relative.\nBenchmark data (Performance): on all seven scenarios (up to 1,000,000 rows), coefficients agree with R to ≤ 2e-14, except the fixed-effects panel (7e-9), where R's stopping rule ends a little earlier. They agree with Stata to between 4e-12 and 2e-7, depending on where Stata's default stopping rule ends.","title":"R"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#statsmodels","page":"Validation","text":"statsmodels 0.15.0, ConditionalLogit:\n\nSAS matched-pairs example: with fit(method=\"newton\") the estimate is 0.9555114450, the same as ConditionalLogit.jl (gconv = 1e-16) to all ten digits, and the standard error agrees to 1e-8 relative (statsmodels uses a numerical Hessian).\nBenchmark data (Performance): with statsmodels' default BFGS, coefficients agree to 1e-5–1e-4. This is the BFGS stopping tolerance, not a difference in the likelihood (see Python statsmodels).","title":"statsmodels"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Internal-checks","page":"Validation","text":"brute-force subset enumeration on 200 random designs;\nForwardDiff gradients and Hessians, including at β = 0;\na 512-bit BigFloat oracle;\ninvariance tests (row order, stratum labels, within-stratum shifts, scaling, duplicated strata, uninformative strata) and closed forms for matched pairs (McNemar);\nthread-count invariance (bitwise identical results).","title":"Internal checks"},{"category":"section","location":"r/#R-survival::clogit","page":"R survival::clogit","text":"This page shows how an R survival::clogit analysis maps onto ConditionalLogit.jl. R's clogit defaults to method = \"exact\", the exact conditional likelihood. Fully converged, it gives the same estimates, standard errors and log-likelihood as ConditionalLogit.jl. The R reference fixtures in the test suite and the Performance benchmarks agree to about 1e-9 or better (see Validation).","title":"R survival::clogit"},{"category":"section","location":"r/#Syntax","page":"R survival::clogit","text":"R ConditionalLogit.jl\nclogit(case ~ x1 + x2 + strata(id), data = df) clogit(@formula(case ~ x1 + x2), df; strata = :id)\nstrata(a, b) strata = [:a, :b]\noffset(o) in the formula offset = :o\ncoxph(Surv(rep(1, n), case) ~ x + strata(id), ties = \"exact\") the same model\nmethod = \"approximate\", \"breslow\", \"efron\" not supported (identical to exact when every stratum has one event)\nweights = ignored by R's exact method; freq = here follows SAS FREQ\nrobust = TRUE, cluster(id) not available for R's exact method; not supported here\ncontrol = coxph.control(iter.max = 50) maxiter = 50\n\nThe response is a 0/1 or logical case indicator in both. R also accepts 1/2 status coding, where 2 is the event; ConditionalLogit.jl needs event = 2 for that.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"r/#Output","page":"R survival::clogit","text":"R output ConditionalLogit.jl\ncoef, se(coef), z, p coeftable(m)\nexp(coef), lower .95, upper .95 (summary(fit)$conf.int) oddsratios(m)\nLikelihood ratio test, Wald test, Score (logrank) test globaltests(m) (.lr, .wald, .score)\nfit$loglik (null, fitted) (nullloglikelihood(m), loglikelihood(m))\nvcov(fit) vcov(m)\nAIC(fit) aic(m)\nBIC(fit) deviance(m) + dof(m) * log(nevents) (see below)\nn, number of events m.nobs_used, number of events in the used rows\nNA coefficient (collinear or constant within strata) coefficient 0, aliased(m)\n\nBIC uses a different n. For coxph fits, including clogit, R's nobs() is the number of events, so BIC(fit) is −2ℓ + p·log(number of events). SAS, Stata and ConditionalLogit.jl use the number of observations. R also counts events in uninformative strata (all cases), which SAS and Stata drop. To get R's value:\n\nnevents = count(==(1), df.case)            # events in all strata, after removing missing rows\ndeviance(m) + dof(m) * log(nevents)\n\nUninformative strata. R keeps strata in which every row is a case or none is. They contribute nothing to the likelihood, but R counts them in n and in the number of events. ConditionalLogit.jl drops them and lists them in stratasummary(m), as SAS and Stata do.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"r/#Convergence","page":"R survival::clogit","text":"R's coxph.control() defaults stop when the relative change in the log-likelihood is below eps = 1e-9, after at most 20 iterations. That usually takes one more Newton step than the SAS GCONV = 1e-8 iterate that ConditionalLogit.jl reports by default. For an exact match, fit with gconv = 1e-16. In the Performance benchmarks, R and the tight fits agree to 1e-14 in the coefficients when every stratum has a fixed number of events, and to 7e-9 on the fixed-effects panel, where R stops a little earlier.","title":"Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"r/#Numerical-range","page":"R survival::clogit","text":"R's exact method works with exp(x'β) directly. Covariates with large values within strata (stratum-level means in the hundreds) can make it overflow, and R then returns NA coefficients without an error. ConditionalLogit.jl centres covariates within strata and computes the likelihood in log space, so it does not overflow. When you compare the two on such data, centre the covariates within strata before fitting in R. The likelihood does not change.","title":"Numerical range"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Module","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Fitting","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Model-Type","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Model Type"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#SAS-style-Results","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"SAS-style Results"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Convergence-and-Diagnostics","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Convergence and Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#StatsAPI-Methods","page":"API Reference","text":"The usual StatsAPI accessors (coef, coefnames, stderror, aic, aicc, bic, deviance, nulldeviance, responsename, weights, islinear) also work on a ConditionalLogitModel. The methods below have behaviour specific to the conditional logit model.","title":"StatsAPI Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API Reference","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit","page":"API Reference","text":"ConditionalLogit\n\nConditional (fixed-effects / matched-set) logistic regression by exact conditional maximum likelihood, written to reproduce SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement (and survival::clogit(method = \"exact\") in R, Stata clogit).\n\nMain entry points: clogit / fit(ConditionalLogitModel, ...), the StatsAPI accessors (coef, stderror, vcov, loglikelihood, aic, bic, ...), and the SAS-table accessors fitstatistics, globaltests, oddsratios, stratasummary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.clogit","page":"API Reference","text":"clogit(formula, data; strata, kwargs...)\nclogit(X, y, strata; kwargs...)\n\nFit a conditional logit model (conditional logistic regression for matched sets). This is the same as fit(ConditionalLogitModel, ...); see its docstrings for the keywords.\n\nm = clogit(@formula(case ~ spontaneous + induced), infert; strata = :stratum)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.clogit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.fit-Tuple{Type{<:ConditionalLogitModel}, FormulaTerm, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"fit(ConditionalLogitModel, formula, data; strata, kwargs...)\nclogit(formula, data; strata, kwargs...)\n\nFit a conditional logit model by exact conditional maximum likelihood, the model fitted by SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement.\n\nformula: a StatsModels @formula(y ~ x1 + x2 + ...). There is no intercept: it is conditioned out with the strata, so y ~ x and y ~ 0 + x are the same model and y ~ 1 + x is an error. Categorical covariates get full-rank (k−1) contrasts.\ndata: any Tables.jl table.\nstrata (required): the column name(s) identifying the strata (matched sets), as a Symbol, a String, or a vector of Symbols or Strings (combined key, like SAS strata a b;). It may also be a vector of stratum values (one per row of data). A vector of Symbols is always read as column names; a vector of Strings is read as column names when every element is a column name and its length differs from the number of rows. strata may also be passed positionally: fit(ConditionalLogitModel, formula, data, strata).\n\nKeywords:\n\nevent: the response level that is the event. The default is true / 1 for Bool or 0/1 responses; any other two-level response requires it. SAS without EVENT= or DESCENDING models the lower value (0); use event = 0 to reproduce such output.\nfreq: FREQ column name or vector. Each row counts as freq identical observations. Non-integers are truncated; rows with freq < 1 are dropped. weights = fweights(v) is accepted as an alias; other weights are rejected.\noffset: offset column name or vector (SAS OFFSET=).\ncontrasts: a Dict of StatsModels contrasts (e.g. EffectsCoding(base = last) to reproduce SAS PARAM=EFFECT).\nstrata_missing = false: treat a missing stratum value as a valid stratum (SAS STRATA ... / MISSING). By default such rows are dropped.\nFitting options: maxiter, gconv, fconv, absfconv, xconv, maxstephalving, singular, dropcollinear, ntasks, sc_n, start, strict, verbose; see FitOptions.\n\nOnly the columns named in the formula, strata, freq and offset are read from data. Rows with a missing value in any of them are dropped (listwise deletion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.fit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.fit-Tuple{Type{<:ConditionalLogitModel}, AbstractMatrix{<:Union{Missing, Real}}, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"API Reference","text":"fit(ConditionalLogitModel, X, y, strata; kwargs...)\nclogit(X, y, strata; kwargs...)\n\nFit a conditional logit model from a covariate matrix X (n×p, no intercept column), a binary response y and a vector of stratum identifiers strata (any hashable values). Rows with missing in X, y, strata, freq or offset are dropped.\n\nstrata may also be given as a keyword: clogit(X, y; strata = s).\n\nKeywords: event, freq, weights (only fweights), offset, strata_missing, coefnames, responsename, plus the fitting options in FitOptions. See clogit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.fit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.FitOptions","page":"API Reference","text":"FitOptions\n\nOptions for the Newton–Raphson fit. The defaults are the SAS PROC LOGISTIC defaults for a conditional (STRATA) analysis. Every field can be passed as a keyword to clogit / fit.\n\nmaxiter = 25: maximum number of Newton–Raphson iterations (SAS MAXITER=).\ngconv = 1e-8: relative gradient criterion g'I⁻¹g / (|ℓ| + 1e-6) (SAS GCONV=).\nfconv, absfconv, xconv (all 0.0, which means off): the SAS FCONV=, ABSFCONV= and XCONV= criteria. As SAS does for STRATA models, an exactly zero change in the log likelihood or in the parameters between iterations also stops the iterations.\nmaxstephalving = 50: maximum number of step-halvings when a Newton step decreases ℓ. Halving also stops once the step is negligible relative to β.\nsingular = 1e7 * eps() ≈ 2.2e-9: tolerance of the in-order collinearity sweep. The default is the SAS SINGULAR= default. SAS tests the sweep pivot of the Hessian; this package tests 1 − R² of each covariate on the earlier ones in the within-stratum centred (FREQ-weighted) Gram matrix. Both have the same null space, but borderline cases can be decided differently.\ndropcollinear = true: set aliased coefficients to 0 (SAS behaviour); false throws.\nntasks = Threads.nthreads(): number of tasks for the per-stratum work (≥ 1). The results are bitwise identical for any value. Fits may themselves run concurrently (e.g. inside Threads.@threads or Threads.@spawn).\nsc_n = :informative: which n enters SC/BIC = -2LogL + p·log(n). :informative counts observations (sum of FREQ) in informative strata, and :all counts every observation used. They differ only when there are uninformative strata.\nstart = nothing: starting values (default 0, as in SAS). They must be finite. If ℓ(start) < ℓ(0), the start is discarded with a warning and the fit starts from 0.\nstrict = false: throw a ConvergenceError instead of warning when the fit did not converge or the data are separated.\nverbose = false: print the iteration history while fitting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.FitOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.ConditionalLogitModel","page":"API Reference","text":"ConditionalLogitModel\n\nA fitted conditional logit model. It is created by clogit or fit(ConditionalLogitModel, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.ConditionalLogitModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.fitstatistics","page":"API Reference","text":"fitstatistics(m) -> FitStatistics\n\nSAS \"Model Fit Statistics\" for a conditional analysis:\n\n-2 Log L = −2ℓ. \"Without Covariates\" is −2ℓ(0) = 2Σk log C(nk, m_k) when there is no offset.\nAIC = −2 Log L + 2p\nSC = −2 Log L + p·log(n), with n = nobs(m), the number of observations (sum of FREQ), not the number of strata. This was checked against the SAS LOGIEX11 output. By default (sc_n = :informative) n counts only observations in informative strata; whether SAS also counts uninformative strata is unverified (sc_n = :all does). The text display prints the n used whenever it differs from the observations used.\n\nHere p = dof(m); \"Without Covariates\" has p = 0. aic(m) and bic(m) return the \"With Covariates\" AIC and SC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.fitstatistics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.globaltests","page":"API Reference","text":"globaltests(m) -> GlobalTests\n\nThe likelihood ratio, score and Wald tests of the global null hypothesis β = 0, each with df = dof(m) (non-aliased parameters), as in SAS:\n\nLikelihood Ratio = 2(ℓ(β̂) − ℓ(0))\nScore = U(0)' I(0)⁻¹ U(0), evaluated at β = 0 (with the offset, if any)\nWald = β̂' I(β̂) β̂ = β̂' V⁻¹ β̂\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.globaltests"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.oddsratios","page":"API Reference","text":"oddsratios(m; level = 0.95) -> OddsRatioTable\n\nOdds ratio estimates with Wald confidence limits (SAS CLODDS=WALD, the default table).\n\nA continuous main effect gets exp(β), for a one-unit increase.\nA categorical main effect gets one row per non-reference level, labelled \"var level vs base\". The estimate is the level-versus-reference contrast computed from the term's contrast matrix, so it is right under effect coding too (SAS PARAM=EFFECT), where exp(β) is not an odds ratio.\nTerms that appear in interactions are omitted, as in SAS.\nModels fitted from a matrix get exp(β) for each non-aliased coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.oddsratios"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.oddsratio","page":"API Reference","text":"oddsratio(m, c::AbstractVector; level = 0.95) -> (estimate, lower, upper)\n\nOdds ratio exp(c'β̂) for a general contrast c (length p), with Wald limits exp(c'β̂ ± z·√(c'Vc)). Aliased coefficients must have zero weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.oddsratio"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.stratasummary","page":"API Reference","text":"stratasummary(m) -> StrataSummary\n\nThe SAS \"Strata Summary\": strata grouped by response pattern (events, non-events), uninformative patterns included. It is a Tables.jl table (DataFrame(stratasummary(m))) with columns pattern, events, nonevents, nstrata, frequency, informative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.stratasummary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.FitStatistics","page":"API Reference","text":"FitStatistics\n\nSAS \"Model Fit Statistics\". Fields aic, sc, m2logl, each a NamedTuple (without, with) for the models without and with covariates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.FitStatistics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.GlobalTests","page":"API Reference","text":"GlobalTests\n\nSAS \"Testing Global Null Hypothesis: BETA=0\". Fields lr, score, wald, each a NamedTuple (chisq, df, pvalue).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.GlobalTests"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.OddsRatioTable","page":"API Reference","text":"OddsRatioTable\n\nSAS \"Odds Ratio Estimates\": columns effect, estimate, lower, upper (Wald confidence limits at level level).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.OddsRatioTable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.StrataSummary","page":"API Reference","text":"StrataSummary\n\nCounts of strata by response pattern (SAS \"Strata Summary\"). Strata in which every observation is an event, or none is, are uninformative. Their conditional likelihood is identically 1, so they are reported here but not used in the analysis.\n\nIt is a Tables.jl table with one row per response pattern and columns pattern, events, nonevents, nstrata, frequency and informative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.StrataSummary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.StrataPattern","page":"API Reference","text":"StrataPattern\n\nOne row of the SAS \"Strata Summary\" table: strata with nevents events and nnonevents non-events (both counted with FREQ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.StrataPattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.ResultTable","page":"API Reference","text":"ResultTable\n\nSupertype of the small result tables (strata summary, fit statistics, global tests, odds ratios). Each implements the Tables.jl column interface, so DataFrame(t) works.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.ResultTable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.converged","page":"API Reference","text":"converged(m)\n\ntrue if a convergence criterion was met and no separation was detected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.converged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.convergencestatus","page":"API Reference","text":"convergencestatus(m) -> Symbol\n\nThe status of the fit: :converged, :maxiter, :stalled, :complete_separation or :quasi_separation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.convergencestatus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.iterations","page":"API Reference","text":"iterations(m)\n\nNumber of Newton–Raphson iterations taken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.iterations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.iterationhistory","page":"API Reference","text":"iterationhistory(m) -> Vector{IterationRecord}\n\nThe SAS-style iteration history: iteration, log-likelihood, relative gradient (GCONV) criterion, step-halvings, ridging and parameter values (full length, 0 for aliased coefficients), for each iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.iterationhistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.IterationRecord","page":"API Reference","text":"IterationRecord\n\nOne Newton–Raphson iteration: iteration number, log-likelihood and relative gradient criterion at the iterate, the number of step-halvings used to reach it, whether the information matrix had to be ridged, and the parameter vector (full length, in the order of coefnames, with 0 for aliased coefficients).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.IterationRecord"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.aliased","page":"API Reference","text":"aliased(m) -> BitVector\n\nCoefficients set to 0 because their covariate is a linear combination of earlier covariates within strata, or is constant within every stratum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.aliased"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ConditionalLogit.ConvergenceError","page":"API Reference","text":"ConvergenceError(msg)\n\nThrown when strict = true and the Newton–Raphson iterations did not converge, or the maximum likelihood estimate does not exist (separation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ConditionalLogit.ConvergenceError"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.vcov-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"vcov(m)\n\nInverse information matrix at β̂, the SAS covariance of the estimates (COVB). Rows and columns of aliased coefficients are NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.vcov"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.informationmatrix-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"informationmatrix(m; expected = true)\n\nInformation matrix at β̂: minus the Hessian of the conditional log-likelihood, which is Var[x_S] summed over strata. It does not depend on the response, so the observed and expected information are the same. Aliased rows and columns are 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.informationmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.score-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"score(m)\n\nGradient of the conditional log-likelihood at β̂ (≈ 0 at convergence). The score test of β = 0 is in globaltests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.score"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.loglikelihood-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"loglikelihood(m)\n\nExact conditional log-likelihood at β̂. Summed over informative strata, with FREQ weighting. -2loglikelihood(m) is SAS \"-2 Log L, With Covariates\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.loglikelihood"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.nullloglikelihood-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"nullloglikelihood(m)\n\nConditional log-likelihood at β = 0 (with the offset, if any). Without an offset it is -Σ_k log C(n_k, m_k). -2nullloglikelihood(m) is SAS \"-2 Log L, Without Covariates\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.nullloglikelihood"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.nobs-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"nobs(m)\n\nThe number of observations used, which is the n in SC/BIC. It is the sum of FREQ over the observations in informative strata (sc_n = :informative, the default), or over all observations used (sc_n = :all). The two differ only if there are uninformative strata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.nobs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.dof-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"dof(m)\n\nNumber of estimated (non-aliased) parameters. There is no intercept and no dispersion parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.dof"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.dof_residual-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"dof_residual(m)\n\nnobs(m) - dof(m). Defined so that StatsModels.lrtest works; the likelihood ratio test itself uses only the deviances and dof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.dof_residual"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.confint-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"confint(m; level = 0.95)\n\nWald confidence limits β̂ ± z·SE (SAS CLPARM=WALD).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.confint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.coeftable-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"coeftable(m; level = 0.95)\n\nStandard StatsBase.CoefTable with estimate, standard error, z, p-value and Wald confidence limits. The SAS \"Wald Chi-Square\" is z², with the same p-value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.coeftable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.modelmatrix-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"modelmatrix(m)\n\nThe covariate matrix of the observations used, in data order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.modelmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.response-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"response(m)\n\nThe event indicator (true = event) of the observations used, in data order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.response"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.fitted-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"fitted(m)\n\nWithin-stratum conditional probability that each observation is an event, given its stratum's number of events: πi = P(i ∈ S). It is the softmax for 1:M matched sets (mk = 1), and Σi fi πi = mk within each stratum. Observations in uninformative strata get 1 (all events) or 0 (no events).\n\nIt is computed on the first call and cached (a fit does not pay for it). The cost is O(Σk nk mk) time and O(rowsk · mk) memory per stratum, where nk counts FREQ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.fitted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.predict-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"predict(m)\npredict(m, newdata; strata, offset = nothing, type = :prob)        # formula models\npredict(m, Xnew; strata, offset = nothing, type = :prob)           # matrix models\n\nWithout new data, predict(m) == fitted(m).\n\nWith new data:\n\ntype = :linpred gives the linear predictor x'β̂ (+ offset). It has no intercept, so it is identified only up to a constant within each stratum.\ntype = :prob gives the within-stratum choice probabilities exp(ηi) / Σ{j ∈ k} exp(η_j), the probability that row i is the event of its stratum (McFadden's conditional logit for new choice sets: one event per stratum).\n\nstrata is a column name (or names) of newdata, or a vector of stratum values. Rows with missing covariates, strata or offset get missing.\n\nResiduals are not defined for this model (residuals gives a MethodError).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.predict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsModels.isnested-Tuple{ConditionalLogitModel, ConditionalLogitModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"StatsModels.isnested(m1, m2; atol = 0)\n\ntrue if m1 is nested in m2: both were fitted to the same observations (response, strata, FREQ and offset), and every non-aliased covariate of m1 lies, within strata, in the span of the covariates of m2. Used by StatsModels.lrtest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsModels.isnested"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#ConditionalLogit.jl-compared-with-R,-Stata-and-Python","page":"Performance","text":"Seven synthetic scenarios were fitted, from 40,000 to 1,000,000 rows. With default settings and one thread, ConditionalLogit.jl was:\n\n7–22× faster than R survival::clogit(method = \"exact\");\n13–41× faster than Stata clogit on one core;\n144–324× faster than Python statsmodels ConditionalLogit.\n\nThe \"best\" comparison uses each program's fastest multi-core configuration: Stata/MP on 16 cores, and ConditionalLogit.jl on 8 or 24 threads. On that basis ConditionalLogit.jl was 18–53× faster than Stata/MP. All three programs give the same coefficients, up to their convergence tolerances (see Agreement).\n\nScenario Rows R clogit Stata clogit, 1 core Stata/MP clogit, 16 cores statsmodels Julia, 1 thread Julia, 8 threads Julia, 24 threads\npairs 40,000 0.14 s 0.48 s 0.52 s 3.72 s 11.6 ms 9.7 ms 18.1 ms\nmatch1to4 100,000 0.69 s 1.20 s 1.02 s 10.01 s 30.9 ms 24.4 ms 47.7 ms\nchoice 500,000 3.41 s 5.01 s 3.73 s 35.68 s 0.22 s 0.15 s 0.22 s\nmulti 200,000 2.61 s 6.96 s 2.91 s 35.02 s 0.18 s 81.4 ms 68.6 ms\npanelfe 200,000 1.69 s 4.81 s 2.60 s 44.59 s 0.15 s 62.1 ms 63.5 ms\nbigstrata 50,000 1.79 s 3.29 s 1.00 s 70.32 s 0.25 s 74.8 ms 56.8 ms\nchoicelarge 1,000,000 11.80 s 18.94 s 11.51 s 108.92 s 0.76 s 0.50 s 0.52 s","title":"ConditionalLogit.jl compared with R, Stata and Python"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Speedup","page":"Performance","text":"Each cell is the other program's time divided by ConditionalLogit.jl's time, so a value above 1× means ConditionalLogit.jl finished first:\n\n\"vs R\", \"vs Stata, 1 core\", \"vs Stata/MP, 16 cores\", \"vs statsmodels\": ConditionalLogit.jl on one thread. R and statsmodels are single-threaded.\n\"Julia best\" columns: ConditionalLogit.jl at its fastest of 1, 8 or 24 threads.\nSettings: every program used its default settings on the same data.\n\nScenario Rows vs R vs Stata, 1 core vs Stata/MP, 16 cores vs statsmodels vs R (Julia best) vs Stata/MP (Julia best) vs statsmodels (Julia best)\n1:1 matched pairs (pairs) 40,000 12.2× 41.4× 44.6× 322× 14.5× 53.0× 383×\n1:4 matching (match1to4) 100,000 22.5× 38.9× 33.0× 324× 28.5× 41.8× 411×\nchoice sets of 10 (choice) 500,000 15.6× 23.0× 17.1× 164× 22.7× 24.8× 237×\n1–9 events per stratum (multi) 200,000 14.8× 39.4× 16.5× 198× 38.1× 42.5× 511×\nfixed-effects panel (panelfe) 200,000 11.0× 31.2× 16.9× 290× 27.3× 41.8× 719×\n100-row strata, 50 events (bigstrata) 50,000 7.3× 13.3× 4.0× 285× 31.5× 17.6× 1237×\nchoice sets of 10, 20 covariates (choicelarge) 1,000,000 15.6× 25.1× 15.2× 144× 23.6× 23.0× 218×\n\nThe narrowest margin is Stata/MP on 16 cores on bigstrata, at 4.0× against single-threaded ConditionalLogit.jl. Ratios for the smallest data sets are noisier, because ConditionalLogit.jl's times there are only 10–30 ms.","title":"Speedup"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Scenarios","page":"Performance","text":"Each scenario is drawn from a conditional logit model with N(0, 1) covariates. Rows are shuffled, so every program has to group them by stratum.\n\nScenario Strata Stratum size Events per stratum Covariates Typical use\npairs 20,000 2 1 5 1:1 matched case–control\nmatch1to4 20,000 5 1 10 1:4 matched case–control\nchoice 50,000 10 1 10 McFadden choice model\nmulti 20,000 10 1–9 10 k₁:k₂ matching\npanelfe 10,000 20 Bernoulli; includes all-0 and all-1 strata 5 fixed-effects logit panel\nbigstrata 500 100 50 5 large risk sets\nchoicelarge 100,000 10 1 20 large choice data","title":"Scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Same-stopping-point","page":"Performance","text":"The three programs stop at different points:\n\nConditionalLogit.jl stops by default at the SAS GCONV = 1e-8 iterate.\nR stops when the relative change in ℓ is below 1e-9.\nStata starts from its own initial values and stops when the scaled gradient is below 1e-5 (e(ic) counts its iterations).\nstatsmodels uses BFGS and stops when the gradient norm of −ℓ/n is below 1e-5. It does not report an iteration count.\n\nThe table below repeats the one-thread comparison with ConditionalLogit.jl fully converged (gconv = 1e-16). That is more accurate than either R or Stata, so this is the conservative comparison: 6–19× faster than R, 11–49× faster than Stata on one core, and 125–385× faster than statsmodels.\n\nScenario R iterations Stata iterations Julia iterations (default / gconv = 1e-16) Julia, gconv = 1e-16 R / Julia Stata / Julia statsmodels / Julia\npairs 4 3 3 / 4 9.7 ms 15× 49× 385×\nmatch1to4 4 2 3 / 4 35.6 ms 19× 34× 281×\nchoice 4 2 3 / 4 0.24 s 14× 21× 147×\nmulti 5 3 4 / 5 0.22 s 12× 32× 161×\npanelfe 4 3 3 / 3 0.16 s 11× 31× 287×\nbigstrata 5 2 4 / 5 0.30 s 6× 11× 233×\nchoicelarge 4 2 3 / 4 0.87 s 14× 22× 125×","title":"Same stopping point"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Agreement","page":"Performance","text":"The table gives the largest absolute difference between each program's coefficients and the fully converged ConditionalLogit.jl fit.\n\nScenario R Stata statsmodels\npairs 2e-15 4e-12 1e-05\nmatch1to4 3e-15 2e-07 2e-05\nchoice 3e-15 5e-10 9e-05\nmulti 5e-15 7e-09 4e-05\npanelfe 7e-09 7e-09 4e-05\nbigstrata 1e-14 1e-09 1e-05\nchoicelarge 2e-14 4e-09 1e-04\n\nThe differences from Stata come from Stata's default stopping rule, not from the likelihood. Refitting match1to4 in Stata with nrtolerance(1e-12) tolerance(1e-14) shrinks the difference from 2e-7 to 4e-10. The same holds for R on panelfe, where R stops a little early. statsmodels' default BFGS stops much earlier, at about 1e-5 (see Python statsmodels); with method=\"newton\" it converges to the same estimates.","title":"Agreement"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Method","page":"Performance","text":"What is timed: a complete fit from data already in memory, through each program's usual interface. That covers grouping, the design matrix, all iterations and the covariance matrix. Reading the CSV file is not timed.\nRepetitions: R and Stata give the median of 3 fits. statsmodels also gives the median of 3 fits, except where a single fit takes longer than 30 s, which is timed once. Its times varied by 10–30% between two runs. ConditionalLogit.jl gives the median of 5 fits after one untimed fit that compiles the code. Times under 0.1 s are noisy at the millisecond level.\nSettings: each program's defaults. R and statsmodels are single-threaded. Stata/MP was run with set processors 1 and with all 16 licensed processors.\nPrecision: Stata reads the CSV files with import delimited, asdouble. Without asdouble it stores the data as single-precision floats, and its estimates then differ in about the 8th digit.\nHost: Intel Core i9-14900HX (8 performance and 16 efficiency cores, 32 hardware threads), Linux, Julia 1.13.0, R 4.6.1 with survival 3.8-6, Stata/MP 15 (16-core licence), Python 3.13.14 with statsmodels 0.15.0.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Interpretation","page":"Performance","text":"One thread: most of the gain comes from the kernels. Strata with one event use a centred softmax, and the general case uses the log-space recursion described in Algorithm. The cost is O(nk · min(mk, nk − mk) · p²) per stratum, and the per-stratum work allocates nothing.\nThreads: threading helps most when the per-stratum work is heavy (multi, panelfe, bigstrata: 2.4–4.4× from 1 to 24 threads). With one event per stratum, the fixed, partly serial cost of grouping and building the design dominates, so the gain is small (choicelarge: 1.5×). On this host, going past the 8 performance cores can slow the smallest problems down.\nstatsmodels runs its recursion in pure Python, one group at a time, which accounts for most of its gap.\nStata/MP gains up to 3.3× from 16 cores on the heavy scenarios. On pairs it is slightly slower with 16 cores than with 1.\nAbsolute times depend on the machine. The ratios show the difference between the implementations on this benchmark; they do not predict the speedup on every data set.","title":"Interpretation"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Reproducing-the-benchmarks","page":"Performance","text":"julia --project=benchmark/compare -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=benchmark/compare benchmark/compare/scenarios.jl         # writes data/*.csv (≈ 570 MB)\nRscript benchmark/compare/r_bench.R                                       # needs survival, data.table\nuv venv benchmark/compare/.venv && uv pip install --python benchmark/compare/.venv/bin/python statsmodels pandas\nbenchmark/compare/.venv/bin/python benchmark/compare/py_bench.py\nfor t in 1 8 24; do julia --project=benchmark/compare -t $t benchmark/compare/julia_bench.jl; done\n(cd benchmark/compare && stata-mp -b do stata_bench.do)                   # Stata 15 or later\njulia --project=benchmark/compare benchmark/compare/report.jl            # Markdown tables\n\nThe raw results behind the tables above are in benchmark/compare/results/.","title":"Reproducing the benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Package-internal-benchmarks","page":"Performance","text":"benchmark/bench.jl times the individual stages (design build, one evaluation of ℓ, score and information, and full fits through the matrix and formula interfaces), and benchmark/run_threads.jl sweeps thread counts and checks that the results are bitwise identical. See Development.","title":"Package-internal benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Comparison-with-SAS,-Stata,-R-and-Python","page":"Overview","text":"ConditionalLogit.jl, SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement, Stata clogit, R survival::clogit and Python statsmodels ConditionalLogit all maximise the same exact conditional likelihood\n\nL(beta) = prod_k fracexpleft(sum_i in textevents_k x_ibetaright)sum_S subset textstratum  k S = m_k expleft(sum_j in S x_jbetaright)\n\nso, fully converged, they give the same estimates, standard errors and log-likelihood. They differ in syntax, defaults, which statistics they print, and how they treat weights, uninformative strata and collinear covariates. This page summarises the differences. The pages for SAS, Stata, R and Python show how to reproduce each program's output, and Performance compares speed.","title":"Comparison with SAS, Stata, R and Python"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#At-a-glance","page":"Overview","text":" ConditionalLogit.jl SAS PROC LOGISTIC + STRATA Stata clogit R survival::clogit Python statsmodels ConditionalLogit\nCall clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id) model y(event='1') = x; strata id; clogit y x, group(id) clogit(y ~ x + strata(id), data = df) ConditionalLogit.from_formula(\"y ~ 0 + x\", groups=\"id\", data=df).fit()\nLikelihood exact exact exact (\"always uses the true conditional likelihood\") exact by default; method = \"approximate\"/\"breslow\"/\"efron\" available exact\nStandard errors inverse of the analytic information matrix inverse information matrix vce(oim) (default) inverse information matrix inverse of a numerical Hessian (finite differences of the score)\nEvent y == 1 / true, or event = lower ordered value (Y = 0) unless EVENT= or DESCENDING any nonzero value TRUE / 1 (status coding) must be coded 0/1\nStarting values β = 0 β = 0 Stata's own β = 0 β = 0\nConvergence SAS GCONV = 1e-8 (reports that iterate); gconv to tighten GCONV = 1e-8 scaled gradient gH⁻¹g' < 1e-5 and (relative change in β ≤ 1e-6 or in ℓ ≤ 1e-7) relative change in ℓ < 1e-9, at most 20 iterations BFGS, gradient norm of −ℓ/n < 1e-5, at most 100 iterations; method=\"newton\" available\nStrata with all events or none dropped; listed in the Strata Summary dropped; listed in the Strata Summary dropped with a note; excluded from Number of obs kept; contribute nothing but count in n and in the number of events dropped with a warning; excluded from nobs\nCovariate constant within strata / collinear coefficient 0, DF 0, warning; aliased(m) parameter set to 0 \"omitted because of no within-group variance\" NA not checked: arbitrary estimates with huge SEs, or no SEs\nGlobal tests LR, score, Wald LR, score, Wald LR LR, Wald, score (logrank) none\nNull log-likelihood nullloglikelihood(m) = ℓ(0) −2 Log L without covariates e(ll_0) fit$loglik[1] not reported (model.loglike(zeros))\nBIC / SC n observations in informative strata (sc_n = :all for all) observations e(N): observations in informative strata number of events not reported\nPseudo R² 1 - loglikelihood(m) / nullloglikelihood(m) not printed McFadden pseudo R² not printed not reported\nOdds ratios oddsratios(m) (Wald CI) Odds Ratio Estimates (Wald CI) , or summary(fit)$conf.int np.exp(r.conf_int())\nFrequency weights freq =: each row counts freq times (SAS FREQ) FREQ: per row fweight: each group counts w times; must be constant within group ignored by the exact method (warning) not supported\nOther weights rejected WEIGHT not allowed with STRATA iweight, pweight ignored by the exact method not supported\nRobust / cluster SE no no vce(robust), vce(cluster id) not available for the exact method no\nOffset offset = OFFSET= offset() offset() in the formula offset=\nThreads yes, bitwise-identical results for any number of threads — Stata/MP no no","title":"At a glance"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Which-numbers-agree","page":"Overview","text":"When all five programs are fully converged on the same data (statsmodels with method=\"newton\"):\n\nIdentical: coefficients, standard errors, z (or Wald χ² = z²), p-values, Wald confidence intervals, odds ratios, log-likelihood, ℓ(0), the likelihood-ratio statistic, AIC.\nIdentical where printed: score and Wald global tests (SAS, R and ConditionalLogit.jl).\nClose: statsmodels standard errors, which come from a numerical Hessian (about 1e-8 relative).\nDifferent by definition:\nBIC: R uses the number of events as n, SAS and Stata the number of observations. Stata excludes observations in uninformative strata; for SAS this is unverified (see SAS PROC LOGISTIC).\nThe number of observations reported: R counts uninformative strata; Stata and statsmodels do not.\nSigns: SAS without EVENT='1' or DESCENDING models Y = 0, which negates every coefficient.\nDifferent by stopping rule: each program stops at a different point, so the last printed digit can differ. ConditionalLogit.jl's default reproduces SAS. With gconv = 1e-16 it reproduces every digit that Stata and R print (see Validation). statsmodels' default BFGS stops much earlier (about the 5th significant digit), so use method=\"newton\" there.","title":"Which numbers agree"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Weights-are-not-interchangeable","page":"Overview","text":"The programs mean different things by frequency weights:\n\nSAS FREQ and freq = here: a row with weight 3 is three identical observations in the same stratum. The stratum grows, and so does its number of events.\nStata fweight: a group with weight 3 is three identical groups. Weights must be constant within each group.\nR clogit(method = \"exact\"): weights are ignored, with a warning.\nstatsmodels: no weights.\n\nTo reproduce a Stata fweight analysis, replicate the groups (see Frequency weights). To reproduce SAS FREQ in R, expand the rows.","title":"Weights are not interchangeable"},{"category":"section","location":"statsmodels/#Python-statsmodels","page":"Python statsmodels","text":"This page shows how a Python statsmodels analysis with statsmodels.discrete.conditional_models.ConditionalLogit maps onto ConditionalLogit.jl. statsmodels computes the exact conditional likelihood for any number of events per group, but by default it maximises it with BFGS and takes standard errors from a numerically differentiated Hessian. Its default estimates therefore agree with the other programs only to about 4–5 significant digits. With method = \"newton\" they agree to the digits that matter.","title":"Python statsmodels"},{"category":"section","location":"statsmodels/#Syntax","page":"Python statsmodels","text":"statsmodels ConditionalLogit.jl\nConditionalLogit.from_formula(\"y ~ 0 + x1 + x2\", groups=\"id\", data=df).fit() clogit(@formula(y ~ x1 + x2), df; strata = :id)\nConditionalLogit(endog, exog, groups=g).fit() clogit(X, y, g)\nConditionalLogit(..., offset=o) offset = :o\n.fit(method=\"newton\") the default (Newton–Raphson)\n.fit(maxiter=200) maxiter = 200\n.fit_regularized(...) (elastic net) not supported\n\nThe formula needs 0 +: statsmodels warns if it is missing, and it rejects a design matrix with an intercept column. ConditionalLogit.jl drops the intercept automatically. endog must be coded 0/1. statsmodels also offers ConditionalMNLogit (multinomial) and ConditionalPoisson, which have no counterpart here.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"statsmodels/#Output","page":"Python statsmodels","text":"statsmodels output ConditionalLogit.jl\ncoef, std err, z, P>|z|, [0.025 0.975] (r.summary()) coeftable(m)\nr.params, r.bse, r.cov_params() coef(m), stderror(m), vcov(m)\nLog-Likelihood (r.llf) loglikelihood(m)\nNo. Observations (r.nobs), No. groups nobs(m), stratasummary(m)\nnp.exp(r.params), np.exp(r.conf_int()) oddsratios(m)\nnot reported: ℓ(0), LR / score / Wald global tests, AIC, BIC nullloglikelihood(m), globaltests(m), aic(m), bic(m)\n\nTo get ℓ(0) in statsmodels, use model.loglike(np.zeros(p)). No. Observations excludes groups with no variation in the outcome, which statsmodels drops with a warning, as Stata does. So r.nobs equals nobs(m).","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"statsmodels/#Differences-that-change-results","page":"Python statsmodels","text":"Stopping rule. The default method = \"bfgs\" stops when SciPy's gradient tolerance (gtol = 1e-5, on −ℓ/n) is met. On the SAS matched-pairs example it returns 0.955478 where the exact maximum is 0.955511, and standard error 0.526231 where the exact value is 0.526235. The difference is about 6e-5 standard errors, so it is invisible in a 3-decimal summary but not in a replication check. Use .fit(method=\"newton\"), which converges to the same value as SAS, Stata, R and ConditionalLogit.jl.\nStandard errors. statsmodels differentiates the analytic score numerically to get the Hessian, so standard errors carry a small finite-difference error. With method = \"newton\", the same example gives 0.52623482 against the exact 0.52623481. ConditionalLogit.jl uses the analytic information matrix.\nCollinearity. statsmodels does not check for redundant covariates:\nWith collinear covariates (for example z = 2 * gall), it returns an arbitrary split of the effect with standard errors in the thousands, and no warning.\nWith a covariate that is constant within every group (for example age in the matched infert data), inverting the Hessian fails and asking for the standard errors raises an error.\nConditionalLogit.jl sets the redundant coefficient to 0, reports it with aliased(m), and warns.\nNumerical range. statsmodels computes exp(x'β) directly. With covariates whose stratum means are in the hundreds, the recursion overflows: statsmodels warns that it did not converge and returns a wrong estimate. Centre the covariates within groups before fitting there. ConditionalLogit.jl does this internally and works in log space.\nSpeed. The recursion runs in pure Python, one group at a time, so large data sets take minutes (see Performance).","title":"Differences that change results"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Tests","page":"Development","text":"julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'                      # full test suite (no R needed)\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(julia_args=`-t 4`)'     # the same, multithreaded\n\nThe R reference fixtures in test/reference/ are produced only by test/reference/generate_reference.R, which needs R with the survival and jsonlite packages:\n\nRscript test/reference/generate_reference.R","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Benchmarks","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=benchmark -t 8 benchmark/bench.jl [choice mixed unequal bigstrata offset stringids]\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/run_threads.jl mixed          # thread sweep, checks bitwise-identical results\n\nThe benchmark environment uses [sources], which needs Julia 1.11 or later. On Julia 1.10, run julia --project=benchmark -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path = \".\")' once first.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Documentation","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe site is built into docs/build/. On every push to main, the Documentation GitHub Actions workflow builds it and deploys it to the gh-pages branch.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Source-layout","page":"Development","text":"File Contents\nsrc/frontend.jl formula and matrix front ends, listwise deletion, event coding, option validation\nsrc/design.jl the stratified design: informative strata as contiguous, within-stratum centred column ranges; collinearity sweep\nsrc/likelihood.jl per-stratum kernels (softmax for one event, log-space recursion otherwise) and the threaded evaluator\nsrc/optimize.jl Newton–Raphson with SAS convergence criteria; separation diagnostics\nsrc/fit.jl glue from design to model\nsrc/inference.jl global tests, fit statistics, odds ratios\nsrc/statsapi.jl StatsAPI and StatsModels methods\nsrc/show.jl the SAS-style text report","title":"Source layout"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#Stata-clogit","page":"Stata clogit","text":"This page shows how a Stata clogit analysis maps onto ConditionalLogit.jl. Stata's clogit uses the exact conditional likelihood for any number of positive outcomes per group, so a fully converged fit gives the same numbers. The examples in the Stata [R] clogit manual entry are reproduced to every printed digit (see Validation).\n\nusing ConditionalLogit, DataFrames","title":"Stata clogit"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#Syntax","page":"Stata clogit","text":"Stata ConditionalLogit.jl\nclogit y x1 x2, group(id) (or strata(id)) clogit(@formula(y ~ x1 + x2), df; strata = :id)\nclogit y x1 i.race, group(id) clogit(@formula(y ~ x1 + race), df; strata = :id, contrasts = Dict(:race => DummyCoding()))\nxtlogit y x, fe (panel variable id) clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id)\nclogit y x, group(id) offset(o) clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id, offset = :o)\nclogit y x, group(id) or oddsratios(m)\nclogit y x, group(id) level(90) coeftable(m; level = 0.90), oddsratios(m; level = 0.90)\nclogit y x, group(id) iterate(50) clogit(...; maxiter = 50)\nclogit y x [fw = w], group(id) replicate the groups (see below)\nvce(robust), vce(cluster c), [pw = ], [iw = ] not supported\n\nEvent coding. Stata treats any nonzero value of the dependent variable as a positive outcome. ConditionalLogit.jl models y == 1 (or true) for 0/1 and Bool responses, and asks for event = otherwise. For a variable coded, say, 0/2, create the indicator first: df.pos = df.y .!= 0.\n\nFactor variables. Stata's i.race uses the lowest level as the base. StatsModels' DummyCoding() does the same, so the parameter table matches. A numeric column is treated as continuous unless you give it a contrast (or convert it to a categorical array), as i. does in Stata.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#Output","page":"Stata clogit","text":"Stata output ConditionalLogit.jl\nNumber of obs (e(N)) nobs(m) (observations in informative strata)\nnote: N groups (M obs) omitted because of all positive or all negative outcomes stratasummary(m); m.nobs_used - nobs(m) observations\nnote: x omitted because of no within-group variance aliased(m); the coefficient is 0\nLR chi2(k), Prob > chi2 (e(chi2)) globaltests(m).lr\nLog likelihood (e(ll)) loglikelihood(m)\ne(ll_0) nullloglikelihood(m)\nPseudo R2 (e(r2_p)) 1 - loglikelihood(m) / nullloglikelihood(m)\nCoefficient, Std. err., z, P>|z|, [95% conf. interval] coeftable(m)\nOdds ratio, [95% conf. interval] (, or) oddsratios(m)\nStd. err. of an odds ratio (, or) exp.(coef(m)) .* stderror(m) (delta method)\ne(b), e(V) coef(m), vcov(m)\nestat ic: AIC, BIC aic(m), bic(m)\n\nestat ic uses N = e(N), the number of observations in the groups that were kept. That is ConditionalLogit.jl's default n for BIC, so bic(m) equals Stata's BIC.\n\nConditionalLogit.jl also reports what Stata does not print by default: the score and Wald global tests (globaltests(m)) and the Strata Summary.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#Convergence","page":"Stata clogit","text":"Stata stops when the scaled gradient gH⁻¹g' is below 1e-5 and either the relative change in the coefficients is at most 1e-6 or the relative change in the log-likelihood is at most 1e-7 (the [R] Maximize defaults). That is usually one Newton step beyond the SAS GCONV = 1e-8 iterate that ConditionalLogit.jl reports by default. Stata prints coefficients to seven significant digits, so the last digit can differ. Use gconv = 1e-16 to reproduce Stata to every printed digit:\n\nm = clogit(@formula(low ~ lwt + smoke + ptd + ht + ui + race), lowbirth2;\n           strata = :pairid, contrasts = Dict(:race => DummyCoding()), gconv = 1e-16)","title":"Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#stata-fweights","page":"Stata clogit","text":"In Stata, fweights apply to groups: a group with weight 3 stands for three identical groups, and the weight must be the same for every observation in the group. ConditionalLogit.jl's freq = follows SAS FREQ instead, where each row stands for freq identical observations in the same stratum. These are different models.\n\nTo reproduce a Stata fweight analysis, replicate each group w times with a new group id. The example below is the weighted McNemar example from the Stata manual: 56 matched pairs entered as four weighted groups.\n\npairs = DataFrame(id      = [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4],\n                  case    = [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0],\n                  exposed = [1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0],\n                  weight  = [8, 8, 22, 22, 8, 8, 18, 18])\n\n# Stata: clogit case exposed [fw = weight], group(id) or\nexpanded = reduce(vcat, [transform(g, :id => (id -> fill(10_000 * id[1] + r, length(id))) => :id)\n                         for g in groupby(pairs, :id) for r in 1:g.weight[1]])\nm = clogit(@formula(case ~ exposed), expanded; strata = :id)\noddsratios(m)\nOdds Ratio Estimates\n───────────┬──────────────────────────────────\n           │     Point     95% Wald\n  Effect   │  Estimate   Confidence   Limits\n───────────┼──────────────────────────────────\n  exposed  │     2.750        1.224    6.177\n───────────┴──────────────────────────────────\n\n\n\nStata prints Odds ratio = 2.75, [95% conf. interval] = 1.224347 6.176763, Number of obs = 112, LR chi2(1) = 6.79, Log likelihood = -35.419282 and Pseudo R2 = 0.0875:\n\nnobs(m), round(globaltests(m).lr.chisq; digits = 2), loglikelihood(m),\nround(1 - loglikelihood(m) / nullloglikelihood(m); digits = 4)\n(112, 6.79, -35.41928183710218, 0.0875)","title":"Frequency weights"},{"category":"section","location":"stata/#Choice-models-(cmclogit)","page":"Stata clogit","text":"Stata's cmclogit (formerly asclogit) fits McFadden's choice model, with one chosen alternative per case. Its likelihood is the conditional logit likelihood with the case as the stratum. By default cmclogit adds alternative-specific constants, and case-specific variables enter through interactions with the alternatives. To reproduce it, put those alternative dummies and interactions in the formula explicitly and use the case id as strata.","title":"Choice models (cmclogit)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithm/#Algorithm","page":"Algorithm","text":"The model uses the exact conditional likelihood. Its denominator is computed with the Gail–Lubin–Rubinstein (1981) recursion in a normalised log-space \"mixture\" form, which carries log B, E[xS] and Var[xS] for each subset size. Every update is a convex combination plus a rank-one PSD term. As a result there is no overflow, even for strata with thousands of events, and the information matrix is positive semidefinite by construction.\n\nOther details:\n\nWhen mk > nk − mk the recursion runs on the complement, so the cost is O(nk · min(mk, nk − m_k) · p²) per stratum.\nStrata with one event (or one non-event) use a two-pass centred softmax in O(n_k p²).\nCovariates are centred within strata before fitting. The likelihood is invariant to this, and it removes cancellation in the information matrix when covariates have large stratum-level means.\nStrata are processed in chunks by several tasks, the most expensive chunks first. The chunk partition depends only on the data, and partial sums are reduced in a fixed order. Results are therefore bitwise identical for any number of threads (julia -t N).\nA single very large stratum (when p ≥ 8, or when its p × p × m_k work tensor would exceed 8 MiB) has its information matrix split by columns over tasks. Every entry is computed by the same operations, so the results stay bitwise identical. Per-task memory does not grow with the largest stratum.","title":"Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial fits conditional logit models to two classic matched case–control data sets, shows how to read the results, and covers the options that matter when you reproduce published SAS output.\n\nusing ConditionalLogit, CSV, DataFrames, StatsModels","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Matched-pairs:-LOGIEX11","page":"Tutorial","text":"The SAS/STAT example LOGIEX11 has 63 matched pairs. s is the pair, y is 1 for the case, and gall and hyper are indicators for gall-bladder disease and hypertension.\n\nla = CSV.read(pkgdir(ConditionalLogit, \"test\", \"reference\", \"la.csv\"), DataFrame)\nm1 = clogit(@formula(y ~ gall + hyper), la; strata = :s)\nConditional logistic regression (exact conditional likelihood;\ncf. SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement)\n\nModel Information\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response Variable                y\n  Probability modeled              y = 1\n  Strata Variable                  s\n  Number of Strata                 63\n  Number of Uninformative Strata   0\n  Frequency Uninformative          0\n  Number of Observations Read      126\n  Number of Observations Used      126\n  Optimization Technique           Newton-Raphson (step-halving; ridge if not PD)\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nStrata Summary\n────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response  │                        Number of\n  Pattern   │  Events   Non-events      Strata   Frequency\n────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  1         │       1            1          63         126\n────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nConvergence criterion (GCONV=1E-8) satisfied.\n\nModel Fit Statistics\n─────────────┬───────────────────────────\n             │     Without         With\n  Criterion  │  Covariates   Covariates\n─────────────┼───────────────────────────\n  AIC        │      87.337       86.788\n  SC         │      87.337       92.460\n  -2 Log L   │      87.337       82.788\n─────────────┴───────────────────────────\n\nTesting Global Null Hypothesis: BETA=0\n────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────\n  Test              │  Chi-Square   DF   Pr > ChiSq\n────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────\n  Likelihood Ratio  │      4.5487    2       0.1029\n  Score             │      4.3620    2       0.1129\n  Wald              │      4.0060    2       0.1349\n────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────\n\nAnalysis of Conditional Maximum Likelihood Estimates\n─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n             │                  Standard         Wald\n  Parameter  │  DF   Estimate      Error   Chi-Square   Pr > ChiSq\n─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  gall       │   1     0.9704     0.5307       3.3432       0.0675\n  hyper      │   1     0.3481     0.3770       0.8526       0.3558\n─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nOdds Ratio Estimates\n──────────┬──────────────────────────────────\n          │     Point     95% Wald\n  Effect  │  Estimate   Confidence   Limits\n──────────┼──────────────────────────────────\n  gall    │     2.639        0.933    7.468\n  hyper   │     1.416        0.677    2.965\n──────────┴──────────────────────────────────\n\n\n\nThere is no intercept: it is conditioned out together with the strata. y ~ x and y ~ 0 + x are the same model, and y ~ 1 + x is an error.","title":"Matched pairs: LOGIEX11"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Reading-the-results","page":"Tutorial","text":"Each SAS table has an accessor. The tables are Tables.jl tables, so they convert to a DataFrame:\n\nDataFrame(fitstatistics(m1))\n\u001b[1m3×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m criterion \u001b[0m\u001b[1m without \u001b[0m\u001b[1m with    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │ AIC        87.3365  86.7878\n   2 │ SC         87.3365  92.4604\n   3 │ -2 Log L   87.3365  82.7878\n\nDataFrame(globaltests(m1))\n\u001b[1m3×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m test             \u001b[0m\u001b[1m chisq   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m df    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pvalue   \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String           \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Likelihood Ratio  4.5487       2  0.102864\n   2 │ Score             4.36201      2  0.112928\n   3 │ Wald              4.00597      2  0.134932\n\nDataFrame(oddsratios(m1))\n\u001b[1m2×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m effect \u001b[0m\u001b[1m estimate \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper   \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ gall     2.63902  0.932575  7.46795\n   2 │ hyper    1.41633  0.676536  2.96507\n\nDataFrame(stratasummary(m1))\n\u001b[1m1×6 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m pattern \u001b[0m\u001b[1m events \u001b[0m\u001b[1m nonevents \u001b[0m\u001b[1m nstrata \u001b[0m\u001b[1m frequency \u001b[0m\u001b[1m informative \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Int64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Bool        \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │       1       1          1       63        126         true\n\nThe StatsAPI accessors work as usual:\n\ncoef(m1), stderror(m1), loglikelihood(m1), aic(m1), bic(m1)\n([0.9704073733243318, 0.3480655529404738], [0.5307308284149901, 0.37696343884482764], -41.393921497968094, 86.78784299593619, 92.46040680983914)\n\nSAS output Julia\nModel Information / Number of Observations Read, Used show(m), m.nobs_read, m.nobs_used, m.freq_used\nStrata Summary stratasummary\nConvergence Status converged, convergencestatus, iterations, iterationhistory\n-2 Log L (Without / With Covariates) nulldeviance(m) / deviance(m)\nAIC, SC aic(m), bic(m); both columns in fitstatistics\nTesting Global Null Hypothesis: BETA=0 globaltests\nAnalysis of Maximum Likelihood Estimates coef(m), stderror(m), coeftable(m)\nOdds Ratio Estimates (Wald CL) oddsratios; general contrasts with oddsratio\nCOVB / Wald CL for parameters vcov(m), confint(m)","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Nested-models","page":"Tutorial","text":"The likelihood-ratio test of nested models is the difference in −2 Log L:\n\nm0 = clogit(@formula(y ~ gall), la; strata = :s)\nlrtest(m0, m1)\nLikelihood-ratio test: 2 models fitted on 126 observations\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n     DOF  ΔDOF    LogLik  Deviance   Chisq  p(>Chisq)\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[1]    1        -41.8268   83.6536                   \n[2]    2     1  -41.3939   82.7878  0.8657     0.3521\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────","title":"Nested models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Several-cases-per-set-and-categorical-covariates:-infert","page":"Tutorial","text":"R's infert data set has 83 matched sets with one or two cases and up to four controls. education is a string column, so it gets full-rank (k − 1) contrasts:\n\ninfert = CSV.read(pkgdir(ConditionalLogit, \"test\", \"reference\", \"infert.csv\"), DataFrame)\nm2 = clogit(@formula(case ~ spontaneous + induced), infert; strata = :stratum)\nConditional logistic regression (exact conditional likelihood;\ncf. SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement)\n\nModel Information\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response Variable                case\n  Probability modeled              case = 1\n  Strata Variable                  stratum\n  Number of Strata                 83\n  Number of Uninformative Strata   0\n  Frequency Uninformative          0\n  Number of Observations Read      248\n  Number of Observations Used      248\n  Optimization Technique           Newton-Raphson (step-halving; ridge if not PD)\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nStrata Summary\n────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response  │                        Number of\n  Pattern   │  Events   Non-events      Strata   Frequency\n────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  1         │       1            1           1           2\n  2         │       1            2          82         246\n────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nConvergence criterion (GCONV=1E-8) satisfied.\n\nModel Fit Statistics\n─────────────┬───────────────────────────\n             │     Without         With\n  Criterion  │  Covariates   Covariates\n─────────────┼───────────────────────────\n  AIC        │     181.559      132.404\n  SC         │     181.559      139.431\n  -2 Log L   │     181.559      128.404\n─────────────┴───────────────────────────\n\nTesting Global Null Hypothesis: BETA=0\n────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────\n  Test              │  Chi-Square   DF   Pr > ChiSq\n────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────\n  Likelihood Ratio  │     53.1542    2       <.0001\n  Score             │     48.4386    2       <.0001\n  Wald              │     31.8371    2       <.0001\n────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────\n\nAnalysis of Conditional Maximum Likelihood Estimates\n───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n               │                  Standard         Wald\n  Parameter    │  DF   Estimate      Error   Chi-Square   Pr > ChiSq\n───────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  spontaneous  │   1     1.9859     0.3524      31.7486       <.0001\n  induced      │   1     1.4090     0.3607      15.2583       <.0001\n───────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nOdds Ratio Estimates\n───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────\n               │     Point     95% Wald\n  Effect       │  Estimate   Confidence   Limits\n───────────────┼──────────────────────────────────\n  spontaneous  │     7.285        3.651   14.536\n  induced      │     4.092        2.018    8.298\n───────────────┴──────────────────────────────────\n\n\n\neducation was used for matching, so it is constant within every stratum and is collinear with the conditioned-out stratum intercepts. The package detects this, sets its coefficients to 0 with DF 0, and warns:\n\nm3 = clogit(@formula(case ~ spontaneous + induced + education), infert; strata = :stratum)\naliased(m3)\n4-element BitVector:\n 0\n 0\n 1\n 1","title":"Several cases per set and categorical covariates: infert"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Matching-SAS-class-coding","page":"Tutorial","text":"SAS PROC LOGISTIC codes CLASS variables with PARAM=EFFECT and the last level as reference, while StatsModels uses dummy coding with the first level as base. Pass contrasts to reproduce the SAS parameter table:\n\nclogit(@formula(y ~ g), df; strata = :id,\n       contrasts = Dict(:g => EffectsCoding(base = \"c\")))   # SAS PARAM=EFFECT, last level \"c\"\nclogit(@formula(y ~ g), df; strata = :id,\n       contrasts = Dict(:g => DummyCoding(base = \"c\")))     # SAS PARAM=REF\n\noddsratios reports level-versus-reference odds ratios computed from the contrast matrix, so they match SAS under either coding.","title":"Matching SAS class coding"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Response-ordering","page":"Tutorial","text":"By default SAS models the probability of the lower ordered response value, which is Y = 0 for a 0/1 outcome, unless the code says event='1' or descending. Use event = 0 to reproduce such output. Every coefficient changes sign, and the fit statistics and tests are unchanged:\n\nm4 = clogit(@formula(y ~ gall + hyper), la; strata = :s, event = 0)\ncoef(m4) ≈ -coef(m1), deviance(m4) ≈ deviance(m1)\n(true, true)","title":"Response ordering"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Matrix-interface","page":"Tutorial","text":"X = Matrix(la[:, [:gall, :hyper]])\nm5 = clogit(X, la.y, la.s)\ncoef(m5) ≈ coef(m1)\ntrue","title":"Matrix interface"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Convergence-and-accuracy","page":"Tutorial","text":"As in SAS, the fit stops when the relative gradient criterion falls below gconv = 1e-8 and reports that iterate without taking one more step. The estimates therefore agree with the exact optimum to about six significant digits, which is all SAS prints. For full accuracy, tighten the criterion:\n\nm6 = clogit(@formula(y ~ gall + hyper), la; strata = :s, gconv = 1e-16)\ncoef(m6) .- coef(m1)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.696364904217432e-7\n 1.3333315912467114e-7\n\nNon-convergence and separation give a warning and converged(m) == false, as SAS does. Pass strict = true to throw a ConvergenceError instead.","title":"Convergence and accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Predictions","page":"Tutorial","text":"fitted(m) gives the within-stratum probability that each observation is an event. predict(m, newdata; strata) gives choice probabilities for new choice sets (one event per stratum), and type = :linpred gives x'β̂:\n\npredict(m1, first(la, 4); strata = first(la.s, 4))\n4-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.5\n 0.5\n 0.5\n 0.5","title":"Predictions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Threads","page":"Tutorial","text":"Strata are processed by several tasks (ntasks, default Threads.nthreads()), and the results are bitwise identical for any number of threads. Start Julia with julia -t auto to use them. Fits can also run concurrently, for example a bootstrap inside Threads.@threads.","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"#ConditionalLogit.jl","page":"Home","text":"Conditional logit models in Julia, estimated by exact conditional maximum likelihood.","title":"ConditionalLogit.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"The conditional logit model (conditional logistic regression) is the standard model for data grouped into strata:\n\nMatched case–control studies: each matched set is a stratum, and the cases are its events.\nFixed-effects (Chamberlain) logit: each panel unit is a stratum, and the unit intercepts are conditioned out of the likelihood.\nMcFadden's choice model: each choice set is a stratum with exactly one chosen alternative.\n\nConditionalLogit.jl is written so that published results can be reproduced, whichever program produced them. It computes the same exact conditional likelihood as SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement, Stata clogit (and xtlogit, fe) and R survival::clogit, and documents how to map each program's syntax, defaults and output onto it. A sentence such as\n\nWe estimated the conditional logit model by maximum likelihood using PROC LOGISTIC in SAS (release 9.3).\n\nneeds no options at all: the defaults follow SAS (starting values, Newton–Raphson convergence rule, fit statistics, global tests, odds-ratio tables), and results print in a SAS-like layout. For Stata, R and Python statsmodels, the comparison pages list the few differences in definitions (BIC, weights, uninformative strata) and how to reproduce them.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Exact conditional likelihood for any number of events per stratum (no Breslow or Efron approximation), computed with an overflow-free log-space form of the Gail–Lubin–Rubinstein recursion\nReproduces SAS, Stata, R and statsmodels: SAS PROC LOGISTIC defaults (start at β = 0, Newton–Raphson with GCONV=1e-8, MAXITER=25) and tables; every printed number of the SAS and Stata manual examples; R survival::clogit(method = \"exact\") to 1e-9 or better\nStatsModels formulas and a matrix interface, any Tables.jl source, categorical covariates, FREQ weights, offsets, and multi-column strata keys\nStatsAPI RegressionModel: coef, stderror, vcov, confint, coeftable, aic, bic, lrtest, predict, …\nDiagnostics: collinearity within strata (aliased coefficients), complete and quasi-complete separation, non-convergence\nMultithreaded over strata, with results that are bitwise identical for any number of threads; fits can run concurrently inside Threads.@threads\nFast: on one thread, 7–22× faster than R survival::clogit, 13–41× faster than Stata clogit and 144–324× faster than Python statsmodels; 18–53× faster than Stata/MP on 16 cores (see Performance)","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"ConditionalLogit.jl is not yet in the General registry. Install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/simoneSantoni/ConditionalLogit.jl\")\n\nOr for development:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(path=\"/path/to/ConditionalLogit.jl\")\n\nJulia 1.10 or later is required.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"The SAS/STAT documentation example LOGIEX11 has 63 matched pairs from the Los Angeles endometrial cancer study. The data ship with the package's tests:\n\nusing ConditionalLogit, CSV, DataFrames\n\nla = CSV.read(pkgdir(ConditionalLogit, \"test\", \"reference\", \"la.csv\"), DataFrame)\nfirst(la, 4)\n\u001b[1m4×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m s     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m y     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m gall  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m hyper \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────\n   1 │     1      1      0      0\n   2 │     1      0      0      0\n   3 │     2      1      0      0\n   4 │     2      0      0      0\n\nm = clogit(@formula(y ~ gall), la; strata = :s)\nConditional logistic regression (exact conditional likelihood;\ncf. SAS PROC LOGISTIC with a STRATA statement)\n\nModel Information\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response Variable                y\n  Probability modeled              y = 1\n  Strata Variable                  s\n  Number of Strata                 63\n  Number of Uninformative Strata   0\n  Frequency Uninformative          0\n  Number of Observations Read      126\n  Number of Observations Used      126\n  Optimization Technique           Newton-Raphson (step-halving; ridge if not PD)\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nStrata Summary\n────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Response  │                        Number of\n  Pattern   │  Events   Non-events      Strata   Frequency\n────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────\n  1         │       1            1          63         126\n────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nConvergence criterion (GCONV=1E-8) satisfied.\n\nModel Fit Statistics\n─────────────┬───────────────────────────\n             │     Without         With\n  Criterion  │  Covariates   Covariates\n─────────────┼───────────────────────────\n  AIC        │      87.337       85.654\n  SC         │      87.337       88.490\n  -2 Log L   │      87.337       83.654\n─────────────┴───────────────────────────\n\nTesting Global Null Hypothesis: BETA=0\n────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────\n  Test              │  Chi-Square   DF   Pr > ChiSq\n────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────\n  Likelihood Ratio  │      3.6830    1       0.0550\n  Score             │      3.5556    1       0.0593\n  Wald              │      3.2970    1       0.0694\n────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────\n\nAnalysis of Conditional Maximum Likelihood Estimates\n─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n             │                  Standard         Wald\n  Parameter  │  DF   Estimate      Error   Chi-Square   Pr > ChiSq\n─────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  gall       │   1     0.9555     0.5262       3.2970       0.0694\n─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nOdds Ratio Estimates\n──────────┬──────────────────────────────────\n          │     Point     95% Wald\n  Effect  │  Estimate   Confidence   Limits\n──────────┼──────────────────────────────────\n  gall    │     2.600        0.927    7.293\n──────────┴──────────────────────────────────\n\n\n\nEvery number in the Model Fit Statistics, Global Null Hypothesis, estimates and odds ratio tables is identical to the SAS output printed in the SAS/STAT documentation for this example. The same model in Stata (clogit y gall, group(s)) and R (clogit(y ~ gall + strata(s))) gives the same estimate, standard error and likelihood-ratio test.\n\ncoeftable(m)\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Coef.  Std. Error     z  Pr(>|z|)  Lower 95%  Upper 95%\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ngall  0.955511    0.526235  1.82    0.0694   -0.07589    1.98691\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Choosing-an-Entry-Point","page":"Home","text":"Use Case Call\nFormula and a table clogit(@formula(y ~ x1 + x2), df; strata = :id)\nStatsAPI style fit(ConditionalLogitModel, @formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id)\nDesign matrix clogit(X, y, strata) (no intercept column)\nSeveral columns form the stratum (SAS strata a b;) clogit(f, df; strata = [:hospital, :set])\nSAS FREQ clogit(f, df; strata = :id, freq = :n)\nSAS OFFSET= clogit(f, df; strata = :id, offset = :o)\nSAS default response ordering (models Y = 0) clogit(f, df; strata = :id, event = 0)\nStata clogit y x, group(id) / xtlogit y x, fe clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id)\nR clogit(y ~ x + strata(id)) clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id)\nstatsmodels ConditionalLogit.from_formula(\"y ~ 0 + x\", groups=\"id\", data=df) clogit(@formula(y ~ x), df; strata = :id)\nMatch Stata or R to every printed digit clogit(f, df; strata = :id, gconv = 1e-16)\n\nSee the Tutorial for worked examples, and Comparison with SAS, Stata, R and Python for the details that matter when you compare with published output from those programs.","title":"Choosing an Entry Point"},{"category":"section","location":"#Performance-snapshot","page":"Home","text":"ConditionalLogit.jl compared with R survival::clogit(method = \"exact\"), Stata clogit and Python statsmodels ConditionalLogit on seven synthetic data sets from 40,000 to 1,000,000 rows. All four programs give the same estimates up to their convergence tolerances.\n\nEach cell is the other program's time divided by ConditionalLogit.jl's time, so a value above 1× means ConditionalLogit.jl finished first:\n\n\"vs R\", \"vs Stata, 1 core\", \"vs Stata/MP, 16 cores\", \"vs statsmodels\": ConditionalLogit.jl on one thread. R and statsmodels are single-threaded.\n\"Julia best\" columns: ConditionalLogit.jl at its fastest of 1, 8 or 24 threads.\nSettings: every program used its default settings on the same data.\n\nScenario Rows vs R vs Stata, 1 core vs Stata/MP, 16 cores vs statsmodels vs R (Julia best) vs Stata/MP (Julia best) vs statsmodels (Julia best)\n1:1 matched pairs (pairs) 40,000 12.2× 41.4× 44.6× 322× 14.5× 53.0× 383×\n1:4 matching (match1to4) 100,000 22.5× 38.9× 33.0× 324× 28.5× 41.8× 411×\nchoice sets of 10 (choice) 500,000 15.6× 23.0× 17.1× 164× 22.7× 24.8× 237×\n1–9 events per stratum (multi) 200,000 14.8× 39.4× 16.5× 198× 38.1× 42.5× 511×\nfixed-effects panel (panelfe) 200,000 11.0× 31.2× 16.9× 290× 27.3× 41.8× 719×\n100-row strata, 50 events (bigstrata) 50,000 7.3× 13.3× 4.0× 285× 31.5× 17.6× 1237×\nchoice sets of 10, 20 covariates (choicelarge) 1,000,000 15.6× 25.1× 15.2× 144× 23.6× 23.0× 218×\n\nAbsolute timings depend on the machine (here an Intel Core i9-14900HX, Julia 1.13, R 4.6.1 with survival 3.8-6, Stata/MP 15, Python 3.13 with statsmodels 0.15). See Performance for the timings, scenarios, method and a run to full convergence.","title":"Performance snapshot"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"tutorial.md\", \"comparison.md\", \"sas.md\", \"stata.md\", \"r.md\", \"statsmodels.md\", \"algorithm.md\", \"validation.md\", \"performance.md\", \"development.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"Gail, M.H., Lubin, J.H., Rubinstein, L.V. (1981). Likelihood calculations for matched case-control studies and survival studies with tied death times. Biometrika, 68(3), 703-707. doi:10.1093/biomet/68.3.703\nBreslow, N.E., Day, N.E. (1980). Statistical Methods in Cancer Research, Volume 1: The Analysis of Case-Control Studies. IARC Scientific Publications No. 32. Lyon: International Agency for Research on Cancer.\nMcFadden, D. (1974). Conditional logit analysis of qualitative choice behavior. In P. Zarembka (Ed.), Frontiers in Econometrics (pp. 105-142). New York: Academic Press.\nChamberlain, G. (1980). Analysis of covariance with qualitative data. Review of Economic Studies, 47(1), 225-238. doi:10.2307/2297110\nSAS Institute Inc. SAS/STAT User's Guide, The LOGISTIC Procedure: Conditional Logistic Regression; Example \"Conditional Logistic Regression for Matched Pairs Data\".\nStataCorp. Stata Base Reference Manual, clogit — Conditional (fixed-effects) logistic regression.\nTherneau, T.M. A Package for Survival Analysis in R (survival), function clogit. CRAN","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"@software{SantoniConditionalLogitJL,\n  author = {Santoni, Simone},\n  title = {ConditionalLogit.jl: Exact Conditional Logit Models in Julia},\n  version = {0.1.0},\n  year = {2026},\n  url = {https://github.com/simoneSantoni/ConditionalLogit.jl},\n  note = {Julia package, version 0.1.0. Source code and documentation at the URL above.}\n}\n\nThe same entry is in CITATION.bib, and a machine-readable CITATION.cff is included in the repository.","title":"Citation"}]
}
